Richard Bos wrote on 10/08/2003 10:09 AM:
> T.Hall@railroad.robin.de (Tina Hall) wrote:
>> Schwa?
> 
> Yes, a schwa. Fancy a German talking about pronunciation and not knowing
> what a schwa is! It's a "mute e", as in "the" or the last syllable "-en"
> in most German verbs. In the IPO designated by an inverted e and in
> ASCII usually by @.

I'm not sure this will help a German to understand schwa, as their '-en'
is unaccented and there's pretty much nothing between 'eb' and 'n' in
'eb(e)n' (schwa elision/Schwa-Tilgung). If they do want to utter such a
word articulately, you would hear (if anything) an almost-'e': ebEn, any
schwa gone without a trace. (And the Sprachgefuehl would tell that they
just inserted 'e', rather than replaced a vowel).

